1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,525
The Science of Rainbows.

2
00:00:02,475 --> 00:00:10,750
A rainbow is an optical and meteorological phenomenon produced by the dispersion of sunlight through water droplets suspended in the atmosphere.

3
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:22,762
When sunlight enters a spherical raindrop near its upper surface, it refracts (bends) at the air-water boundary, reflects off the interior rear surface of the drop, and refracts again upon exiting.

4
00:00:22,762 --> 00:00:39,562
This process separates white sunlight into its constituent wavelengths because the refractive index of water varies slightly with frequency: shorter wavelengths (violet, ~400 nm) bend the most, while longer wavelengths (red, ~700 nm) bend the least.

5
00:00:39,562 --> 00:00:49,725
The primary rainbow appears as a circular arc centered on the antisolar point—the point directly opposite the Sun—at an angular radius of approximately 42 degrees.

6
00:00:49,725 --> 00:00:53,937
Red appears on the outermost edge of the arc and violet on the innermost.

7
00:00:53,937 --> 00:01:02,812
A secondary rainbow, fainter and about 10 degrees wider (52-54 degrees), sometimes appears outside the primary bow.

8
00:01:02,812 --> 00:01:11,487
It results from two internal reflections within each droplet, which reverses the color order: red appears on the inside and violet on the outside.

9
00:01:11,487 --> 00:01:21,825
The dark region between the primary and secondary bows is called Alexander's band, named after the 2nd-century Greek philosopher Alexander of Aphrodisias who first described it.

10
00:01:21,825 --> 00:01:27,412
Light in this region undergoes total internal reflection angles that cannot reach the observer.

11
00:01:27,412 --> 00:01:36,537
Rainbows are not physical objects; they are a full-circle cone of light centered on the observer's line of sight, so each person sees their own unique rainbow.

